<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest (plan 4.3). Required since May 2024.

  Three claims, all of them true of this app as the Android app is true of it:

    * NSPrivacyTracking false and no tracking domains. There is no analytics
      SDK, no advertising SDK and no crash-reporting SDK in this app, and the
      licences test fails the build if one ever appears.
    * NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes empty. Everything the app sends goes to the
      household's own server, which Coders Farm never sees; problem reports go
      by the person's own email, from their own mail app. Apple's definition
      of "collected" is data leaving the device in a way that lets US or a
      partner of ours get at it, which is nothing here.
    * The required-reason APIs below, each with a reason code from Apple's
      published list.

  When any of that stops being true — a phone lookup to Wikipedia, an HTTPS
  report destination of ours, any service of ours — this file and the App
  Store privacy label change in the same batch, and the privacy policy the
  same day, as for Play.

  Every reason code below was read from Apple's own published list on
  2026-10-28 (NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons, under App Privacy
  Configuration) and quoted, not paraphrased. One of the four was wrong
  before that check: see the disk-space entry.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  UserDefaults. Settings, the chosen theme, the device name, the phone
		  bar's layout: the app's own preferences and nothing else. CA92.1
		  confirmed correct on 2026-10-28: "to read and write information that
		  is only accessible to the app itself". The app is in no app group and
		  shares nothing, so 1C8F.1 (App Group) does not apply.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  File timestamps. The Files screen shows when a file was changed, the
		  audiobook and video downloads resume from a partial file by its date
		  (with ETag and Last-Modified), and saved media is listed by date.
		  C617.1 confirmed correct on 2026-10-28: "to access the timestamps,
		  size, or other metadata of files inside the app container, app group
		  container, or the app's CloudKit container". Everything we read the
		  timestamp of, we wrote ourselves. DDA9.1 (displaying timestamps) and
		  3B52.1 (files the person picked) would be separate reasons; add
		  3B52.1 when the picked-folder music source lands in I2.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Free disk space. The model download (2.5 GB for Gemma 4 E2B) checks
		  there is room before it starts, and so does saving an audiobook or a
		  video to the phone: a download that fills someone's phone and then
		  fails is worse than a download that never starts.

		  This said 85F4.1 until it was checked against Apple's list on
		  2026-10-28. That was WRONG: 85F4.1 is "to DISPLAY disk space
		  information to the person using the device", which is not what we do.
		  The right code is E174.1, "to check whether there is sufficient disk
		  space to write files", whose own wording carries the exception we
		  rely on: "allows the app to avoid downloading files from a server
		  when disk space is insufficient."

		  E174.1 requires that the app "behave differently based on disk space
		  in a way that is observable to users" — ours does: it says so and
		  does not start the download. If a screen ever SHOWS free space, add
		  85F4.1 beside this one; displaying it is a separate reason.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  System boot time. Used only to measure elapsed time that a clock
		  change cannot bend: a timer counting down, a playback position, the
		  back-off between socket reconnections. Nothing about it leaves the
		  phone. 35F9.1 confirmed correct against Apple's list on 2026-10-28:
		  "to measure the amount of time that has elapsed between events that
		  occurred within the app or to perform calculations to enable timers",
		  and its one exception — elapsed time between in-app events may be
		  sent off-device — is the only thing we would ever send.

		  Still worth re-checking at the first build: if nothing in the app
		  ends up touching `systemUptime` or `mach_absolute_time` because every
		  measurement goes through `ContinuousClock` / `DispatchTime`, this
		  whole entry comes out. Declaring a category we do not use is not
		  rejected, but it is a claim we should not make idly.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
